{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ag5AtbTPA7nuTpY8yWimSU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — GNU ln prompts even when stdin is /dev/null. It always prompts and reads from stdin. If stdin is /dev/null (EOF), it reads nothing, which is treated as decline.\n\nBut the SRD says:\n> When stdin is not a terminal (detected via os.Stdin.Fd() isatty check), treat as decline (do not prompt, do not remove).\n\nSo the SRD explicitly says to NOT prompt when stdin is not a terminal. This is different from actual GNU ln behavior, but the SRD is the spec we follow.\n\nWait, but the test will be differential against gln. Let me re-read the SRD more carefully:\n\n\nThe -i flag requires prompting on stderr with the format 'ln: replace 'DEST'? ', reading a response from stdin, and only proceeding with removal if the response starts with 'y' or 'Y'. When stdin isn't a terminal, I should skip the prompt entirely and treat it as a decline. When both -f and -i are specified, whichever appears last on the command line takes precedence.\n\nI'm realizing there's a discrepancy in how I'm handling the interactive decline case — my implementation returns an error message that gets printed to stderr, but GNU ln silently exits with code 1 without printing anything when the user declines. I need to distinguish between a true error and a user decline so the decline doesn't produce an error message.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":310,"cache_read_input_tokens":36025,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":310},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64f379ca-8127-473c-b4f2-b8d36f5c5b6f","uuid":"639fd361-4bee-4435-b729-bf55ebcb159f"}
