{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014h1756gcdRp2N8rodM8SVW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So GNU ln:\n1. Shows the prompt on stderr when there's a conflict\n2. When declined, doesn't print any extra error message\n3. Continues to the next target (ftb was linked successfully)\n4. Exits 1 if any operation was declined/failed\n\nSo the decline from interactive should: not print an error, not create the link, but set exit code to 1, and continue processing.\n\nI need to change my approach. Instead of returning an error from `handleExisting`, I should use a sentinel value or a different mechanism to signal \"declined\" vs \"error\". \n\nOne approach: return a special error type that `run()` can check, and if it's a \"declined\" error, don't print it but still set exit code 1.\n \nActually, I think the cleaner solution is to have `handleExisting` return a boolean indicating whether to proceed, separate from error handling — if it returns false with no error, that's a declined operation, so I skip printing anything but still mark the exit code as failed.\n\nRefactoring `handleExisting` to return `(proceed bool, err error)` would let me distinguish between \"user declined\" (proceed=false, err=nil) and actual errors (err!=nil), then handle each case appropriately in the main loop. Writing error handling logic... Implementing the helper function...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1011,"cache_read_input_tokens":36335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1011},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64f379ca-8127-473c-b4f2-b8d36f5c5b6f","uuid":"c296b4a4-41f6-48be-9930-1fc8e5ff62fe"}
